/*!
\page ipc-integration.html
\title Qt Interface Framework Inter-Process Communication Integration

An Inter-Process Communication (IPC) enables APIs to be split into multiple processes. Each process
runs independently, sometimes on a separate host machine, and the communication is done using an
IPC. The IPC also allows for multiple clients, or frontends to access the same service. These
frontends can be, for example, the main Infotainment System UI, or different applications that run
within the UI or mobile devices.

\section1 Benefits of using an IPC

An IPC enables functionality distribution and information sharing, between multiple processes or
even between multiple host machines. Separate processes improve the overall reliability of a
complex system and make it possible to separate computation-intensive code from the UI code.
Additionally, backends that use an IPC can be accessible via multiple frontend points, be it the
central UI, separate applications, or even mobile devices or third party applications.

Combined with QtInterfaceFramework's \l{Use the Generator}{automatic code generation} and uniform
feature loading, developing distributed features requires less development overhead, with more
interchangeability and transparency for the UI code.

The Qt Interface Framework Generator makes it possible to generate Middleware Backends using
different IPC mechanisms. The generated backend plugins are loaded and used in the same way as
any other QtInterfaceFramework plugin and are interchangeable with any other backend
implementation with the same API. Since all method calls over QtInterfaceFramework feature
interfaces are asynchronous, switching between IPC mechanisms is transparent to the frontend code.

The Qt Interface Framework Generator currently supports the following IPC mechanisms:

\section1 Qt Remote Objects

The Qt Interface Framework Generator can generate Middleware Backends that use the Qt Remote Objects
module as communication channel. The generated code is split between a client and a server part.
The client-side code is generated as a QtInterfaceFramework backend plugin and as a Middleware
Service, either a server stub or a full simulation server. The client is generated using the
\l{backend-qtro-template} {backend_qtro} template; the server using the
\l{server-qtro-template} {server_qtro} template or \l{server-qtro-simulator-template}
{server_qtro_simulator}. Both the plugin and the server contain generated C++ code and an
intermediate \c{.rep} file that is further processed by Qt's \l {Qt Remote Objects Compiler}
{replica compiler} to produce the actual source and replica classes.

The generated plugin is buildable and usable as-is; you don't have to make any changes. Your task
is then to implement the intended functionality on the server-side. To help you get started with
the server, there's a generated class included, which initializes the connection. All connection
parameters to the plugin and to the server are passed over a config file, either in a default
location or at a user-specified path.

For a steb-by-step introduction to remote backends, see the
\l {interfaceframework/remote}{Qt Interface Framework Generator Remote Example}.

\section1 MQTT

The Qt Interface Framework Generator can generate Middleware Backends that use the
QtMqtt module to communicate via the MQTT protocol. The client-side code is generated
as a QtInterfaceFramework backend plugin using the \l{backend-mqtt-template}{backend_mqtt}
template.

The generated plugin subscribes to MQTT topics to receive property values and signal
events, and publishes to topics to set property values and invoke operations. Each
property, signal, and operation is mapped to a configurable MQTT topic, controlled via
\l{config_mqtt}{@config_mqtt} annotations in the QFace IDL file or its accompanying
YAML file.

The generated plugin is loaded and used in the same way as other QtInterfaceFramework
plugins and is interchangeable with any other backend implementation with the same API.

Unlike the QtRemoteObjects-based templates, the \c backend_mqtt template does not
generate a corresponding server. The MQTT broker and the server-side implementation
must be provided separately.

For a complete example, including a small stand-in device that plays the server role
on the broker, see the \l{Qt Interface Framework - Smart Plug (MQTT)}{Smart Plug example}.

*/
